# Design System Usage

Use `@senler/ui` as the primary UI foundation.

- If `@senler/ui` has a suitable component, use it instead of creating a custom equivalent.
- Prefer existing imports such as `Button`, `Card`, `CardContent`, `InputField`, `Alert`, `AlertDescription`, `Badge`, `Sheet`, `Select`, `Switch`, `Textarea`, and `PageLoader`.
- Compose layout with Tailwind classes around `@senler/ui` components instead of replacing them.
- Prefer semantic Tailwind tokens already used in the app: `bg-card`, `text-foreground`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, `text-destructive`, `bg-primary`, `text-primary-foreground`, `bg-muted`, and `bg-accent`.
- Use component variants and props before adding custom color or shape classes.
- Do not add new UI libraries for design tasks.
- Do not hardcode new hex, RGB, or arbitrary color values unless the project already defines the token and the user explicitly asks for that brand value.

Good: adjust a `Button` variant, a `CardContent` layout, or Tailwind spacing around existing `@senler/ui` components.
Bad: replace a `Button` with a raw `<button>` or add a new custom form control when `InputField`/`Textarea` fits.
